=== refevents example ===
// Command refevents measures how long a record takes to become readable in
// another clone, when the reader is woken by the remote's own ref-change events
// rather than by a poll.
//
// Two clones of one store, in one process, on opposite sides of the wire:
//
//	writer ──append──▶ local refs ──push──▶ remote ──ref event──▶ reader
//	                                          │                     │
//	                                          └───────fetch─────────┘
//
// The point is what the reader does *not* do. It never polls the remote on a
// timer, and it never reads a value out of an event. It waits to be told that
// something moved, then fetches and folds — so the stream is a latency
// optimisation over a correct-but-slow loop, not a source of truth. That is what
// lets it stay correct under a signal that coalesces bursts, replays no history,
// keeps no cursor, and re-snapshots on reconnect. See events.go.
//
// # How the number is arrived at
//
// The writer puts the time it is about to commit into the record itself; the
// reader subtracts that from the time the value became visible in its read
// model. Both readings come from one process on one machine, so the subtraction
// is a duration and not a comparison of two clocks — which is the only reason
// this is worth measuring this way. Split the two halves across machines and the
// number would be measuring NTP skew as much as anything else.
//
// What it therefore includes, end to end: a local commit, a push to the remote,
// the remote's own ref-event fan-out, the reader's fetch, and the fold into a
// read model. The second half of that — from being woken to the value being
// visible — is timed separately and reported alongside, because it is the half
// that is this library's to answer for. The rest is the network and the host.
//
// This is the remote sibling of `mise run latency`, which measures the same
// question without a wire: how long a write takes to become readable in SQL in
// the clone that wrote it.
//
// Run it against a real repository:
//
//	go run ./refevents \
//	  -repo et/your-org/your-repo \
//	  -remote entire://aws-ap-southeast-2.entire.io/et/your-org/your-repo \
//	  -token "$(entire auth token -j au)"
//
// It writes real records under refs/strata/<store>/ and prints what the reader
// sees. -clean removes them again on the way out.
